WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.560
As fans of Chicago Fire now know, Jesse Spencer&nbsp;
has left the building — Firehouse 51, that is. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:11.680
the fourth episode of the show's tenth season, his&nbsp;
character, Matthew Casey, goes to Oregon only to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:16.320
find that Andy Darden's sons aren't doing&nbsp;
too well. After that, he has a heart-to-heart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.480
conversation with Christopher Herrmann, in&nbsp;
which he basically says he is going to leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:25.840
Chicago to help take care of them. In a subsequent&nbsp;
episode, Casey deals with the fallout from that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:31.680
decision — including his burgeoning romance with&nbsp;
Brett — before saying a final goodbye to the crew.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:36.400
Spencer is one of the original Chicago Fire&nbsp;
cast members, having logged 200 episodes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:41.840
the show since it started airing in 2012. While&nbsp;
his character is supposedly going away for three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:46.400
years, there are no guarantees that the actor&nbsp;
will be back at all. After such a long time on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.640
the series, though, fans may be wondering&nbsp;
why the actor has decided to leave now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:55.920
With the cat out of the bag and his exit secured,&nbsp;
Spencer has explained his reasoning to the media.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:00.480
In a recent video interview with Us Weekly,&nbsp;
Spencer said that he actually wanted to leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:05.520
the show between its ninth and tenth seasons,&nbsp;
but creator Derek Haas talked him into staying a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:10.800
few more episodes so he'd have 200 of them on his&nbsp;
résumé. As it turned out, his reasons for leaving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:15.280
the show were entirely personal. Notably,&nbsp;
Spencer has been in network television for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:20.720
18 years straight, which is obviously a long&nbsp;
time whichever way you cut it. Spencer said:

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:24.160
"There's other things that I&nbsp;
would love to do in the future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:27.840
and there's some family that&nbsp;
I need to take care of."

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:32.560
He added that he felt like his departure&nbsp;
storyline worked for him, saying:

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:35.200
"Casey's leaving and going to Oregon, therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:40.000
for the right reasons — it&nbsp;
all felt really organic."

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.000
But will Spencer return to the show&nbsp;
at some point? It's possible. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.840
it sounds like talks have already begun to&nbsp;
bring him back to tie up some loose ends.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:52.320
During the same interview, Haas was asked if&nbsp;
Spencer might come back for a special occasion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:56.480
and he gave an answer that should have&nbsp;
fans cautiously optimistic, saying:

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:00.160
"I'm hoping. I never try to predict&nbsp;
what's going to happen production-wise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:03.680
but we're in the camp of whenever Jesse&nbsp;
wants to come do an episode with us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:06.320
we will move mountains and&nbsp;
Earth to make that happen."

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:10.160
Spencer clarified that he plans to stay&nbsp;
in Chicago with his wife even though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.480
he's stepping away from the show, which is a&nbsp;
little ironic — as the city's harsh climate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:17.920
had him questioning whether to remain&nbsp;
on the series during its early years.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:22.800
Obviously, 200 episodes on one TV show is&nbsp;
a monumental achievement for any actor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:26.480
and something few in the profession&nbsp;
will ever actually experience. It's also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:30.640
something that Spencer himself thought might&nbsp;
not happen in the early days of Chicago Fire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:34.720
Cast members leave shows or get let go&nbsp;
for any number of reasons, ranging from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:38.800
issues in their personal life to decisions&nbsp;
made by the writers. For Spencer, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:44.080
it was the notoriously frigid Chicago winters that&nbsp;
had him unsure about his future on the series.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:48.720
During an interview with Stage Right Secrets&nbsp;
about the 200th episode of Chicago Fire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:52.400
Spencer hailed the show's crew as&nbsp;
integral to the series' longevity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.920
While discussing the hard work the&nbsp;
crew regularly performed on the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.920
he made special mention of Chicago's&nbsp;
cold temperatures. Spencer said:

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:03.200
"I mean, the weather. I didn't&nbsp;
think I'd last a year on the show.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:06.560
After those winters, I was like,&nbsp;
'I don't know if I can do this.'"

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:10.080
Even though he plans to stay in Chicago&nbsp;
now that he has turned in his badge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:13.600
Spencer did indicate that he might&nbsp;
start booking vacations for himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:16.720
during the winter. During his&nbsp;
Us Weekly interview, he said:

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:20.560
"I still am in Chicago right now. I'm not&nbsp;
running off to Los Angeles [...] although I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:25.200
might escape for a little bit of the winter. I&nbsp;
might snowbird, that's what I hear people do."

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.440
Wherever he goes, here's hoping he doesn't stray&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:30.800
too far from the Windy City&nbsp;
— or the set of Chicago Fire.

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:34.800
"I'm gonna miss it, I'm stepping&nbsp;
away, but I'll be back."

